# Pinned third-party artefacts for the Visiblaze SCA action.
#
# ── Why this file exists rather than a version string in action.yml ──
#
# The action downloads and EXECUTES Trivy on a customer's runner, next to their source and
# whatever credentials that job holds. We are the security vendor in that transaction, so
# "fetch the latest release" is not available to us: it means whatever appears at a URL we do
# not control becomes code we told a customer to run. A pinned version plus a verified digest
# means an upstream compromise, a re-tagged release, or a MITM changes the digest and the job
# stops instead of executing.
#
# Keeping the pin in its own file, rather than inline, makes a version bump a reviewable
# one-file diff with the digest visibly changing alongside the URL — the two must never move
# independently.
#
# ── The sentinel, and why the action refuses to run without real values ──
#
# `UNSET` is the sentinel. action.yml checks for it and fails closed, so this action cannot
# run with an unverified dependency, and any pin reset to UNSET disables the action until it
# is filled again. The Trivy digests below were cross-checked against
# trivy_0.70.0_checksums.txt as published by the upstream release.
#
# To change a pin:
#
#   1. Pick the exact release tag, never "latest".
#   2. Download each asset and compute its digest on a machine you trust:
#        curl -fsSL -o trivy.tar.gz "<asset url>"
#        sha256sum trivy.tar.gz
#      Cross-check against the checksums file the upstream release publishes. Trivy DOES
#      publish one, so there is no judgement call here — if the two disagree, stop.
#   3. Replace the value. Do not reformat: action.yml sources this file with `set -a`, so
#      every line must stay a plain KEY=value with no spaces around `=`.
#   4. Verify the guard still works by reverting one value to UNSET and confirming the action
#      refuses to run.
#
# The SCANNER_SHA256_* values are this action's OWN binary, built reproducibly from
# cmd/scanner/visiblaze-sca in the Visiblaze backend with:
#
#   CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux GOARCH=<arch> go build -trimpath \
#     -ldflags "-s -w -buildid= -X main.version=<tag>" -o visiblaze-sca_linux_<arch>
#
# -trimpath and an empty -buildid are what make the digest depend on the source rather than on
# whose machine built it. Two runs of that command on the same commit produce byte-identical
# binaries — measured, both architectures.
#
# But be precise about who can check it. The command above does NOT pass -buildvcs=false, so the
# binary embeds the commit it was built from and whether that tree was dirty; dropping the flag
# changes the digest (measured: 6eca6f4e... with VCS state, 8ca17f56... without). So these two
# numbers are reproducible from the SAME commit of a clean tree, and the backend they are built
# from is private. A customer cannot regenerate them, and the README should not imply otherwise.
# What a customer can verify is that the digest here matches the asset they downloaded, which is
# the check that actually protects them.
#
# Release builds must therefore come from a clean tree at a recorded commit. v0.1.2 was built
# from visiblaze-IQ-Backend 8d123885.
#
# ── What is NOT pinned here, and why ──
#
# The Visiblaze binary is resolved from SCANNER_VERSION plus the checksums.txt published in
# that same release. It is our own artefact, so there is one digest to trust and we generate
# it. Trivy is somebody else's, which is exactly why it is pinned by hand.
#
# ── The vulnerability database is deliberately NOT pinned, and that is a real trade-off ──
#
# Trivy downloads its own ~1.27 GiB vulnerability database at scan time, and pinning that
# would defeat the point: yesterday's database cannot know about today's advisory. So the
# thing this action is actually asking a customer to trust is the CODE (pinned) and not the
# DATA (necessarily fresh).
#
# The consequence worth stating plainly, because it is the failure mode of architecture A: a
# Trivy database that is present but expired scans successfully and reports nothing. Measured
# on 0.70.0 — an expired DB exits 0 with zero findings. The action therefore reads Trivy's own
# metadata.json and reports the database's age alongside the findings, so a silent stale scan
# arrives as a stated fact rather than as a clean bill of health.

# ── Our own scanner release ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A full base URL rather than a derived one: the action is also run from an in-repo path during
# CI (uses: ./public/...), where github.action_repository is empty, so deriving it would work in
# the published case and silently break the case that proves the published case.
SCANNER_RELEASE_BASE=https://github.com/Visiblaze/visiblaze-sca-scan/releases/download
SCANNER_VERSION=v0.1.2

# The scanner's own digests, pinned HERE rather than fetched at run time.
#
# The first draft downloaded checksums.txt from the same release as the binary and verified
# against that. It reads like a real check and is not one: whoever can replace a release asset
# can replace the checksum file beside it, so it proves only that the download was not
# corrupted in transit — which TLS already covers.
#
# A digest in this file is committed to git and moves only through a visible change, which is
# the same guarantee TRIVY_SHA256_* below already relies on. Both halves of the action are now
# pinned the same way.

# ── Trivy (architecture A) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TRIVY_VERSION=0.70.0
TRIVY_URL_LINUX_AMD64=https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/releases/download/v0.70.0/trivy_0.70.0_Linux-64bit.tar.gz
TRIVY_SHA256_LINUX_AMD64=8b4376d5d6befe5c24d503f10ff136d9e0c49f9127a4279fd110b727929a5aa9
TRIVY_URL_LINUX_ARM64=https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/releases/download/v0.70.0/trivy_0.70.0_Linux-ARM64.tar.gz
TRIVY_SHA256_LINUX_ARM64=2f6bb988b553a1bbac6bdd1ce890f5e412439564e17522b88a4541b4f364fc8d
SCANNER_SHA256_LINUX_AMD64=6eca6f4e9d729a114b81440bcc78837ae2f227c8205d628bc9f85265f3015481
SCANNER_SHA256_LINUX_ARM64=5635f6e79e05b08dd3404c2b13705da3110be2f6aa6af8d39fe21222487c9361
